{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1538506713,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUzNjYwMzUwMTI=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2890","origin":"github"},"title":"\"edit\" or even \"open\" construct","message":"This discussion would not be new, previous ones:\n- #378 (Brainstorming for \"git annex unlock\" use) - boiled down to use unlock/lock pair\n- #453 (initial title is irrelevant) - has [joey's comment](https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/453#issuecomment-220850060) on `git annex adjust --unlock`\n\nI still think that in particular with Python API it would be a big convenience to the users to be able to manipulate file content in basic operations without doing all the manual get/unlock/lock/... combination.  E.g.  something like\n\n## 'w' mode:\n\n```python\nwith ds.open(\"filename\", \"w\") as f:\n   f.write(\"new content replacing the old one even if it was not present\")\n```\nwhich would do \n- `rm filename`\n- `open(\"filename\", \"w\")`\n- ... (context) \n- `git annex add \"filename\"`\n\n## \"a\" (`b` or not)\n\nit would \n- `get`\n- `unlock`\n- `open(\"filename\", \"a\")`;\n- ...\n- `git annex add ...`\n\n## 'r'\n\n- `get`\n- `open`\n\nin `w` and `a` mode if possible/needing hiding any v5 vs v6 peculiarities (i.e. locking it back so real symlink is committed and not git reference file).\n\nWhat do you think?","files":null}]}